this was a written work autobiographical  that you helped to adapt for the stage  you played many yourself yes how tough  was it to make this into a film it's  been a long journey it's based on a  graphic novel like you said that I  didn't write but I first adapted into a  stage play and then into the film and  I've been working on the project for  eight years all told it's been a big  passion project something that I've  never felt kind of so passionate about a  project before in my life so each phase  was really difficult and I learned so  much about about the story as I went  along and all of the different  incarnations I did but it was great too  I got to know these characters so  intimately I'm getting sad now that my  journey with this story is almost over  actually this has been compared to by  some people and you credit catch her in  the Ryan this a kind of female Catcher  in the Rye well at what you intended I  never really set out to to make it a  female Catcher in the Rye I just had  this feeling when I read this book that  this character was so honest and so real  and was I related to her it was how I  felt when I was a teenage girl and I  just remember thinking this must be what  boys feel like when they read Catcher in  the Rye they have this character who  makes them feel like all of their dark  thoughts that they've had or all of the  things that they've experienced when  they were a teenager probably makes is  not so out of the ordinary they're  probably pretty normal the paradox of  this is that the the girl is 15 years  old you've made a movie about a girl who  couldn't be able to see the movie about  herself because of the rating for this  so your audience part of your audience  in a way is out of reach who do you see  your audience and how how what have you  been hearing in response early on well  when I read the book Phoebe's book I was  in my late 20s but it's still resonated  with me as I think there's something  about teenagehood  it takes a really long time to get over  our teenager it took me a long time  items working out here on the screen but  so I feel like that that time of life is  so potent and you go through so much and  it's so the emotions are so extreme and  I  think we get over it quickly so I feel  like for me and I think for a lot of  women who I've talked to this still  feels like us it still feels really real  and like if it's our story  I do hope teenage girls can see the  movie it's it's got a rated R here so  girls can go if they have an adult  bringing them at least and which is  better than in the UK it's got an 18  rating and people can't go even if they  have an adult with them they can't go at  all so it's a stricter rating there but  I I hope it's a movie that's not just  for women - that men can see themselves  in and relate to this female protagonist  I've been relating to male protagonists  for my whole life so because that's kind  of all it was on offer yeah talk a  little bit about the casting you have an  English actress for the lead yes you  have Kristen Wiig who met whom you know  known for a long time who everyone knows  is a great comic actress yes and  Alexander Skarsgard those that's the  triumvirate yes how did the casting come  about well I never thought I was gonna  cast somebody who was an American for  the lead actress but um Belle Polly who  is so incredible in the film she  submitted an audition tape and in her  audition tape she did her American  accent I didn't realize she was British  until she got to the end and she put a  little personal message in and went into  her normal dialect and she recorded just  this really sweet personal message  explaining how much the film meant to  her and she had never read anything that  she cared about in this way and she just  asked me to have a conversation with her  so it was such a random thing to get an  audition tape and I don't think people  often get cast from audition tapes but  it was so compelling and she was so good  that we started a Skype relationship and  then eventually I got her into New York  Goddard in New York and I had her and  Alexander sit down in a room and we  worked on a couple scenes and their  chemistry was so right that then that's  how that came together but yeah it's an  incredible cast and all three of and  Kristen I I was friends with Kristen  Wiig which I feel like such a jerk  whenever I say this like such a  douchebag  um I was I knew Kristen because my  husband worked for Saturday Night Live  for a long time so I had thought of her  for this part because I thought it was  such an interesting take such an  interesting choice  so different for her and I knew she was  wanting to do more dramatic work and she  was starting to take on really  interesting projects so I approached her  with it and I think I I think I gave her  the script and I was like just say no  you probably don't want to do it just  it's fine you don't have to do it at all  and I said just don't even reply and  then she read the script and really  liked it so that was a huge as I was  watching I think I thought okay Kristen  weeks so I thought I'm going to be  waiting for her to break out and do  something funny but after her first  scene I didn't really feel that way I  feel like she's just good you just buy  this as somebody stepping right out of  the 70s and we all knew that Mott that  mom who was not the best mom doing coke  with their daughters and stuff like that  at least I did I grew up in the Bay Area  and there were a lot of parents like  that and and then there's also the fact  that Alexander Skarsgard is committing a  felony yeah and so you're watching it  you're watching this girl come into her  own sexually but at the same time here's  the creepy 35 year old guy how the  balance and getting the performances to  work and that must have been difficult  yeah it was tricky it felt like a  tightrope walk kind of the entire time  of directing the movie but the the movie  i